# Mount a card as soon as it is inserted, unmount when it is pulled.
#
# The helper decides for itself whether a device is removable (anything that
# is not the system or user-data partition from the kernel cmdline), so this
# rule can afford to be broad.
#
# ============ WHAT %k IS FOR, AND WHY IT WENT WRONG ============
#
# These rules match EVERY block partition on the phone, and they always did --
# the breadth is deliberate, because the helper is the thing that knows what a
# card is. What was not true is that the helper read the name they pass:
# do_remove() ignored it and unmounted the card for any partition removal
# anywhere. That became load-bearing the day the helper learned to format,
# because partprobe's BLKRRPART makes the kernel remove and re-add every
# partition on the disk -- so a format fires this rule at itself, mid-format,
# and the `remove` published `state=absent` over the `mounted` the format had
# just written. The phone said "Card formatted and ready." and then could not
# see the card until the next reboot.
#
# The helper now compares %k against the card it published and does nothing for
# anything else, and the whole dispatcher runs under an flock so these handlers
# cannot land inside a format at all. RUN is still fine here -- the helper
# mounts and returns -- but "returns immediately" is no longer the reason, the
# lock is.
ACTION=="add", SUBSYSTEM=="block", ENV{DEVTYPE}=="partition", RUN+="/NeoDCT/System/hw/neodct-sdcard add %k"
ACTION=="add", SUBSYSTEM=="block", KERNEL=="mmcblk[0-9]", RUN+="/NeoDCT/System/hw/neodct-sdcard add %k"
ACTION=="remove", SUBSYSTEM=="block", ENV{DEVTYPE}=="partition", RUN+="/NeoDCT/System/hw/neodct-sdcard remove %k"
ACTION=="remove", SUBSYSTEM=="block", KERNEL=="mmcblk[0-9]", RUN+="/NeoDCT/System/hw/neodct-sdcard remove %k"
